{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise his heel.</b> Most of the commentators said that the evil inclination draws its power from the primordial serpent, and the evil inclination resembles at first a spider's thread and afterward the ropes of a wagon. Therefore it says 'he shall bruise your head' — for if a person is zealous and goes out to do battle against his evil inclination at the head, that is to say, at the beginning, immediately when it comes upon him, then 'he shall bruise you,' for a person will defeat it easily and snap it off himself, just as a spider's thread is snapped. However, if at the head of its words he gives room to his inclination and follows its counsel day after day until it grows strong like the ropes of a wagon, then it is hard to defeat, and on the contrary, it will defeat him. Therefore it is said to the serpent, 'and you shall bruise his heel,' for the heel means the end, and He meant by this that if at the end he wished to do battle against you, then you shall bruise him and defeat him, for it will be hard for a person to snap the ropes of a wagon from upon himself, since the evil inclination is already bound within him with ropes of love through indulgences, and it is hard for this defiled and uncircumcised one to separate himself. And this is also a parable about the repentance a person does in his youth, that is, at the head of his days — it is easy for him to leave his way. However at the heel, at the end, when he is already habituated in his deeds, even if he grows old he will not turn away from it, and then 'you shall bruise him.' And this may also be said of the grave transgressions — idolatry, forbidden sexual relations, and bloodshed, and their like — your inclination will not be able to lead you into them so easily. What simpleton would turn aside here, and who would listen to it, to commit these principal transgressions? But at the heel — that is, the minor commandments that a person treads with his heels — 'you shall bruise him,' for it will be easy for you to lead him into them. For the way of the evil inclination is that it begins at the heel and climbs and creeps upward until the head, like the venom of the serpent, which begins at the heel and goes and creeps upward until the head. So too the evil inclination begins by leading a person into the minor commandments that people tread with their heels, and after this one transgression leads to another, and it ascends from the minor to the grave, until his iniquities pass over his head, as it says (Psalms 38:5), 'for my iniquities have passed over my head.' And this is a parable about the transgressions that are the head of all transgressions. Therefore it says (Lamentations 1:9), 'her filthiness was in her skirts, she did not remember her end.' For the one who is wise will take to heart that when the evil inclination comes to defile him at his skirts — that is, in a lowly place like the heel — it will rise from there and creep upward and its end will be bitter, for it will lead him from the minor to the grave. And the wicked will not understand this, but the wise will understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:26:58.511301504Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}